WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Telemetry is the operational evidence that shows what a system is doing,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.440
how well it is performing, and where it may be failing.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.380
In ordinary services, telemetry often means metrics, logs, traces, events, audit records,

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:12.380
and health checks.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.030
Those signals help teams understand latency, errors, throughput, resource use,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.700
and important state changes.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.720
AI systems need those same signals, but they also need AI-specific context.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.142
A production inference path may depend on a model version, prompt template,

00:00:25.142 --> 00:00:29.180
retrieval index, data pipeline, accelerator, queue, cache, model gateway,

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.280
and policy decision.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.300
If telemetry only says that an API was slow,

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.375
the team still needs to know whether the delay came from model execution, retrieval,

00:00:36.375 --> 00:00:39.320
scheduling, storage, network, or another dependency.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.880
Hybrid and clustered environments make this more important.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.940
A single request can cross application code, orchestration layers, GPU nodes,

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.120
managed services, private networks, and external APIs.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.337
Each layer can be healthy by itself while the combined workflow still fails for users.

00:00:53.337 --> 00:00:57.805
Useful telemetry connects application behavior to infrastructure behavior so engineering,

00:00:57.805 --> 00:01:01.320
operations, security, and governance teams can reason from shared evidence.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.720
Good telemetry is designed before incidents happen.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.120
It is not a pile of everything a platform could emit.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.480
It is a set of signals chosen because they answer operational questions: what happened,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.480
where did it happen, who or what was involved, how severe was it,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:13.490
and what should the team do next?
